module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_recorded_oracle_compared_over_a_live_population

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BelowCeiling, FoundOnRung, RecurringFailureMode, RungRead }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { Mitigatable, MechanicallyPreventable }
import gunbc.rung_climb { ClimbableButUnbuilt }

data a_recorded_oracle_compared_over_a_live_population: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_recorded_oracle_compared_over_a_live_population" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungRead {
    found_at: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable },
    standing: BelowCeiling {
      current: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable },
      ceiling: MechanicallyPreventable,
      blocker: ClimbableButUnbuilt,
      trigger_capability: "a witness-side lens that refuses a claim comparing a recorded literal to a value folded over a repository population the claim does not supply (a data roster read through a production module, such as fleet_reach_probe_targets), enrolled with a fixture red that maps a live roster into a pinned string and a control that maps a supplied roster",
    },
  },

  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: a claim pins bytes recorded once over a closed population (the hosts enrolled at the recording) but re-derives them over the live population every time it runs. The recording is a sound oracle, an independent referent from a past head, and the comparison is not: the left side is an open population, the right side a closed one, so the claim is a change detector for the roster rather than a check of the interface it names. gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed (a live repository population may be compared to a literal only when the literal is grounded in a controlled fixture; completeness is an identity join, not an equality) and section 3 (a witness discriminates at one interface; its inputs are supplied values).",

    "HARM: every correct enrollment turns the claim red while no byte of the subject changed, the red is found later by whoever runs the file (nothing on the required path evaluates a witness, gunbc.recurring_failure_mode a_witness_population_reds_on_main_with_nothing_gating_it), and the repair reflex is to re-record the pin with the new host -- which keeps the change detector and buys the next red. The quieter harm: while red, the pin no longer distinguishes a shaper regression from a roster change, so a real regression landing in that window is invisible.",

    "SPECIMEN (2026-10-24, snappy-cat-148): test.claim.fleet.fleet_ssh_target_address_witness every_enrolled_host_renders_byte_identically_at_port_22 rendered over map(fleet_reach_probe_targets) and compared to bytes recorded over six hosts at the S2(b1) head and re-recorded by gunbc#14108. gunbc#14762 enrolled mtcollins1 (git log -S 'host: operator_host_mtcollins1' on gunbc.fleet.fleet_enrollment names only it), and the claim went red on main: the live render carried one extra host block and, with that block removed, was byte-identical to the recording. The repair supplies the recorded (host, endpoint) population at the crossing, keeps the live roster in the inhabitance claims that resolve every enrolled host through the real producer, and adds a recorded -> enrolled join so a recorded host's endpoint cannot drift past the pin unseen.",

    "SPECIMEN (2026-11-25, warm-gull-237): the SAME claim, one rung deeper, and the repair this ledger names was NOT taken. gunbc#15073 supplied the recorded population and the claim went green, then went red again on main at 5611ac47e730 -- with no byte of either shaper changed -- because two spans INSIDE the recorded bytes are still minted from the live enrollment registry: the UserKnownHostsFile word carries the digest fleet_known_hosts_registry_identity takes over known_hosts_file_rows, in both arms of every host, and the key arm ControlPath carries a socket name whose preimage carries that same registry identity plus the anchor materialized content identity. known_hosts_file_rows is enrollments plus fabric-rail rows plus first-contact rows, so gunbc#16802 landing a scanned host key and gunbc#16868 emptying the first-contact hop list renumbered all twelve digest spans and all six socket names at once. The cheap test answers yes: a correct enrollment reds the claim while shape_fleet_ssh_exec and shape_fleet_ssh_password_exec_trusting are untouched, and a re-record would have bought the next red exactly as the HARM row predicts. The repair takes the two digest-valued spans out of the pinned bytes and reads each back from the ONE authority that mints it -- gunbc.fleet.fleet_known_hosts_anchor fleet_known_hosts_registry_identity for the anchor-path digest and fleet_ssh_multiplex_control_path for the socket -- so every byte around them stays pinned by the recording; it refuses rather than comparing when a recorded span is missing from the pin at its expected count, and test.claim.fleet.fleet_ssh_target_address_witness red_a_perturbed_non_digest_word_is_refused requires the comparison to refuse a perturbed BatchMode word and a perturbed User word, which is the control that keeps a comparison whose two sides both read the same authority from passing tautologically. The digest values themselves stay pinned where they are owned: test.claim.fleet.fleet_known_hosts_anchor_witness w_a_different_registry_is_a_different_registry for the registry digest discriminating, and test.claim.fleet.fleet_ssh_multiplexing_witness red_two_attempts_never_share_a_control_socket for the socket name.",

    "RECOGNITION RULE. In a claim that compares to a recorded literal, find what the compared value is folded over. If it is a roster the claim imports from production (a data row of hosts, modules, sites) rather than a list the claim supplies, the claim is this class. The cheap test: would a correct addition to the roster turn the claim red without any change to the function the claim is named for? If yes, supply the population and move the live roster to a claim that asserts a property of every member, never bytes of the whole.",

    "RUNG found at 1, mitigatable: per-file review and whoever happens to run the witness catch it. ATTAINABLE CEILING 2, mechanically preventable: whether a compared expression's closure folds over an imported data roster is decidable over the accepted tree, but a lens cannot tell a roster that is the claim's subject from one that is only its input, so the refusal needs a declared exemption and stays a lens, not construction.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
